#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Isang libreng kursong IBM sa quantum information at computation"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: sidebar_label
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Panimula"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the following video, John Watrous steps you through the content in this "
"lesson on entanglement in action. Alternatively, you can open the [YouTube "
"video](https://youtu.be/GSsElSQgMbU?list=PLOFEBzvs-VvqKKMXX4vbi4EB1uaErFMSO)"
" for this lesson in a separate window. [Download the "
"slides](https://ibm.box.com/public/static/9a7pjqxfa2d5uucdlhgid625reuw0b2n.pdf)"
" for this lesson.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa sumusunod na video, inaakay ka ni John Watrous sa nilalaman ng araling "
"ito tungkol sa entanglement sa aksyon. Bilang alternatibo, maaari mong "
"buksan ang [YouTube video](https://youtu.be/GSsElSQgMbU?list=PLOFEBzvs-"
"VvqKKMXX4vbi4EB1uaErFMSO) para sa araling ito sa hiwalay na window. "
"[I-download ang mga "
"slide](https://ibm.box.com/public/static/9a7pjqxfa2d5uucdlhgid625reuw0b2n.pdf)"
" para sa araling ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<IBMVideo id=\"134056223\" title=\"In this video, John Watrous introduces "
"quantum entanglement. He further demonstrates how entanglement is leveraged "
"in so-called quantum teleportation. Finally, he describes how entanglement "
"enables superdense coding.\"/>\n"
msgstr ""
"<IBMVideo id=\"134056223\" title=\"Sa video na ito, ipinakilala ni John "
"Watrous ang quantum entanglement. Ipinakita rin niya kung paano ginagamit "
"ang entanglement sa tinatawag na quantum teleportation. Sa huli, "
"inilalarawan niya kung paano pinapahintulutan ng entanglement ang superdense"
" coding.\"/>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this lesson we'll take a look at three fundamentally important examples.\n"
"The first two are the *quantum teleportation* and *superdense coding* protocols, which are principally concerned with the transmission of information from a sender to a receiver.\n"
"The third example is an abstract game, called the *CHSH game*, which illustrates a phenomenon in quantum information that is sometimes referred to as *nonlocality*.\n"
"(The CHSH game is not always described as a game.\n"
"It is often described instead as an experiment — specifically, it is an example of\n"
"a *Bell test* — and is referred to as the *CHSH inequality*.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa araling ito, titingnan natin ang tatlong pundamental na mahahalagang halimbawa.\n"
"Ang una at pangalawa ay ang mga protokol ng *quantum teleportation* at *superdense coding*, na pangunahing may kinalaman sa pagpapadala ng impormasyon mula sa isang nagpapadala patungo sa isang tatanggap.\n"
"Ang ikatlong halimbawa ay isang abstract na laro, na tinatawag na *CHSH game*, na naglalarawan ng isang penomenon sa quantum information na minsan ay tinutukoy bilang *nonlocality*.\n"
"(Ang CHSH game ay hindi palaging inilalarawan bilang isang laro.\n"
"Madalas itong inilalarawan bilang isang eksperimento — partikular, ito ay isang halimbawa ng\n"
"isang *Bell test* — at tinutukoy bilang *CHSH inequality*.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Quantum teleportation, superdense coding, and the CHSH game are not merely examples meant to illustrate how quantum information works, although they do serve well in this regard.\n"
"Rather, they are stones in the foundation of quantum information.\n"
"Entanglement plays a key role in all three examples, so this lesson provides the first opportunity in this course to see entanglement in action, and to begin to explore what it is that makes entanglement such an interesting and important concept.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang quantum teleportation, superdense coding, at ang CHSH game ay hindi lamang mga halimbawa na nagsisilbing ilustrasyon ng kung paano gumagana ang quantum information, kahit na magaling silang gawin ito.\n"
"Sa katunayan, sila ay mga pundasyon ng quantum information.\n"
"Ang entanglement ay may mahalagang papel sa lahat ng tatlong halimbawa, kaya ang araling ito ay nagbibigay ng unang pagkakataon sa kursong ito na makita ang entanglement sa aksyon, at simulan ang pag-explore kung ano ang ginagawang kawili-wili at mahalagang konsepto ang entanglement.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before proceeding to the examples themselves, a few preliminary comments "
"that connect to all three examples are in order.\n"
msgstr ""
"Bago lumipat sa mga halimbawa mismo, may ilang paunang komento na "
"kumokonekta sa lahat ng tatlong halimbawa na dapat banggitin.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Alice and Bob {#alice-and-bob}"
msgstr "Alice at Bob {#alice-and-bob}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Alice* and *Bob* are names traditionally given to hypothetical entities or agents in systems, protocols, games, and other interactions that involve the exchange of information.\n"
"While these are human names, it should be understood that they represent abstractions and not necessarily actual human beings — so Alice and Bob might be expected to perform complex computations, for instance.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang *Alice* at *Bob* ay mga pangalan na tradisyonal na ibinibigay sa mga hypothetical na entity o ahente sa mga sistema, protokol, laro, at iba pang interaksyon na kinabibilangan ng pagpapalitan ng impormasyon.\n"
"Bagaman ito ay mga pangalang pantao, dapat maunawaan na kumakatawan sila sa mga abstraksiyon at hindi kinakailangang tunay na tao — kaya maaaring inaasahang magsagawa ng mga kumplikadong kalkulasyon si Alice at Bob, halimbawa.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These names were first used in this way in the 1970s in the context of cryptography, but the convention has become common more broadly since then.\n"
"The idea is simply that these are common names (at least in some parts of the world) that start with the letters A and B.\n"
"It is also quite convenient to refer to Alice with the pronoun \"her\" and Bob with the pronoun \"him\" for the sake of brevity.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga pangalang ito ay unang ginamit sa ganitong paraan noong 1970s sa konteksto ng cryptography, ngunit naging pangkaraniwan na ang kaugaliang ito nang mas malawak mula noon.\n"
"Ang ideya ay simple lamang — ito ay mga karaniwang pangalan (kahit sa ilang bahagi ng mundo) na nagsisimula sa mga titik A at B.\n"
"Maginhawa rin na tumukoy kay Alice gamit ang panghalip na \"siya (babae)\" at kay Bob gamit ang \"siya (lalaki)\" para sa breviedad.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By default, we imagine that Alice and Bob are in different locations.\n"
"They may have different goals and behaviors depending on the context in which they arise.\n"
"For example, in *communication,* meaning the transmission of information, we might decide to use the name Alice to refer to the sender and Bob to refer to the receiver of whatever information is transmitted.\n"
"In general, it may be that Alice and Bob cooperate, which is typical of a wide range of settings — but in other settings they may be in competition, or they may have different goals that may or may not be consistent or harmonious.\n"
"These things must be made clear in the situation at hand.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa default, inaakala nating ang Alice at Bob ay nasa magkaibang lokasyon.\n"
"Maaari silang magkaroon ng iba't ibang layunin at gawi depende sa konteksto kung saan sila lumitaw.\n"
"Halimbawa, sa *komunikasyon*, na nangangahulugang pagpapadala ng impormasyon, maaaring piliin nating gamitin ang pangalang Alice para sa nagpapadala at Bob para sa tatanggap ng anumang impormasyong ipinapadala.\n"
"Sa pangkalahatan, maaaring makipagtulungan si Alice at Bob, na karaniwan sa malawak na hanay ng mga sitwasyon — ngunit sa iba pang sitwasyon maaari silang mag-kompetensya, o maaari silang may magkaibang layunin na maaaring magkaayon o hindi.\n"
"Ang mga bagay na ito ay kailangang linawin sa sitwasyong pinag-uusapan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can also introduce additional characters, such as *Charlie* and *Diane*, as needed.\n"
"Other names that represent different personas, such as *Eve* for an eavesdropper or *Mallory* for someone behaving maliciously, are also sometimes used.\n"
msgstr ""
"Maaari rin tayong magpakilala ng karagdagang mga karakter, tulad ng *Charlie* at *Diane*, kung kinakailangan.\n"
"Ang iba pang mga pangalan na kumakatawan sa iba't ibang persona, tulad ng *Eve* para sa isang nang-eespiya o *Mallory* para sa isang kumikilos nang may masamang layunin, ay ginagamit din minsan.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Entanglement as a resource {#entanglement-as-a-resource}"
msgstr "Entanglement bilang isang resource {#entanglement-as-a-resource}"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Recall this example of an entangled quantum state of two qubits:\n"
msgstr ""
"Alalahanin ang halimbawang ito ng isang entangled quantum state ng dalawang "
"qubit:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\phi^+ \\rangle =\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 00\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 11\\rangle.\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\phi^+ \\rangle =\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 00\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 11\\rangle.\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It is one of the four Bell states, and is often viewed as the archetypal "
"example of an entangled quantum state.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ito ay isa sa apat na Bell state, at madalas na tinitingnan bilang "
"arketipong halimbawa ng isang entangled quantum state.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We also previously encountered this example of a probabilistic state of two "
"bits:\n"
msgstr ""
"Natagpuan din natin dati ang halimbawang ito ng isang probabilistic state ng"
" dalawang bit:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 00 \\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 11 \\rangle.\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} \\vert 00 \\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} \\vert 11 \\rangle.\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It is, in some sense, analogous to the entangled quantum state $(1).$\n"
"It represents a probabilistic state in which two bits are correlated, but it is not entangled.\n"
"Entanglement is a uniquely quantum phenomenon, essentially by definition:\n"
"in simplified terms, entanglement refers to *non-classical* quantum correlations.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa ilang paraan, ito ay kahalintulad ng entangled quantum state $(1).$\n"
"Kumakatawan ito sa isang probabilistic state kung saan ang dalawang bit ay magkaugnay, ngunit hindi ito entangled.\n"
"Ang entanglement ay isang natatanging quantum na penomenon, talaga na sa kahulugan nito:\n"
"sa simpleng salita, ang entanglement ay tumutukoy sa *non-classical* na quantum correlations.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, defining entanglement as non-classical quantum correlation is somewhat unsatisfying at an intuitive level, because it's a definition of what entanglement is in terms of what it is not.\n"
"This may be why it's actually rather challenging to explain precisely what entanglement is, and what makes it special, in intuitive terms.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa kasamaang palad, ang pagbibigay-kahulugan sa entanglement bilang non-classical quantum correlation ay medyo hindi kasiya-siya sa intuitive na antas, dahil ito ay isang kahulugan ng kung ano ang entanglement batay sa kung ano ang hindi ito.\n"
"Maaaring ito ang dahilan kung bakit talagang mahirap ipaliwanag nang tumpak kung ano ang entanglement, at kung ano ang nagpapaespesyal dito, sa intuitive na paraan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Typical explanations of entanglement often fail to distinguish the two states $(1)$ and $(2)$ in a meaningful way.\n"
"For example, it is sometimes said that if one of two entangled qubits is measured, then the state of the other qubit is somehow instantaneously affected; or that the state of the two qubits together cannot be described separately; or that the two qubits somehow maintain a memory of each other.\n"
"These statements are not false, but why are they not also true for the (unentangled) probabilistic state $(2)$ above?\n"
"The two bits represented by this state are intimately connected: each one has a perfect memory of the other in a literal sense.\n"
"But the state is nevertheless not entangled.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga tipikal na paliwanag ng entanglement ay madalas na hindi nagtatangi ng dalawang state $(1)$ at $(2)$ sa makahulugang paraan.\n"
"Halimbawa, minsan ay sinasabi na kung ang isa sa dalawang entangled qubit ay sinusukat, ang state ng isa pang qubit ay agad na naaapektuhan sa anumang paraan; o na ang state ng dalawang qubit nang magkasama ay hindi maaaring ilarawan nang hiwalay; o na ang dalawang qubit ay parang nagpapanatili ng memorya ng isa't isa.\n"
"Ang mga pahayag na ito ay hindi mali, ngunit bakit hindi rin totoo ang mga ito para sa (hindi entangled) na probabilistic state $(2)$ sa itaas?\n"
"Ang dalawang bit na kinakatawan ng state na ito ay malapit na magkaugnay: bawat isa ay may perpektong memorya ng isa pang bit sa literal na kahulugan.\n"
"Ngunit ang state ay hindi pa rin entangled.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One way to explain what makes entanglement special, and what makes the quantum state $(1)$ very different from the probabilistic state $(2),$ is to explain what can be done with entanglement, or what we can see happening because of entanglement, that goes beyond the decisions we make about how to represent our knowledge of states using vectors.\n"
"All three of the examples to be discussed in this lesson have this nature, in that they illustrate things that can be done with the state $(1)$ that cannot be done with *any* classically correlated state, including the state $(2).$\n"
msgstr ""
"Isang paraan upang ipaliwanag kung ano ang nagpapaespesyal sa entanglement, at kung ano ang nagpapaiba ng quantum state $(1)$ mula sa probabilistic state $(2),$ ay ang ipaliwanag kung ano ang maaaring gawin gamit ang entanglement, o kung ano ang maaari nating makita na nangyayari dahil sa entanglement, na lampas sa mga desisyon na ginagawa natin tungkol sa kung paano kumakatawan ang ating kaalaman ng mga state gamit ang mga vector.\n"
"Lahat ng tatlong halimbawa na tatalakayin sa araling ito ay may ganitong katangian, dahil naglalarawan sila ng mga bagay na maaaring gawin gamit ang state $(1)$ na hindi maaaring gawin gamit ang *kahit anong* classically correlated state, kabilang ang state $(2).$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Indeed, it is typical in the study of quantum information and computation that entanglement is viewed as a resource through which different tasks can be accomplished.\n"
"When this is done, the state $(1)$ is viewed as representing one *unit* of entanglement, which we refer to as an *e-bit*.\n"
"The \"e\" stands for \"entangled\" or \"entanglement.\"\n"
"While it is true that the state $(1)$ is a state of two qubits, the quantity of entanglement that it represents is one e-bit.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa katunayan, karaniwan sa pag-aaral ng quantum information at computation na ang entanglement ay tinitingnan bilang isang resource kung saan ang iba't ibang gawain ay maaaring maisakatuparan.\n"
"Kapag ginawa ito, ang state $(1)$ ay tinitingnan bilang kumakatawan sa isang *unit* ng entanglement, na tinutukoy natin bilang isang *e-bit*.\n"
"Ang \"e\" ay nakatayo para sa \"entangled\" o \"entanglement.\"\n"
"Bagaman totoo na ang state $(1)$ ay isang state ng dalawang qubit, ang dami ng entanglement na kinakatawan nito ay isang e-bit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Incidentally, we can also view the probabilistic state $(2)$ as a resource, which is one bit of *shared randomness*.\n"
"It can be very useful in cryptography, for instance, to share a random bit with somebody (presuming that nobody else knows what the bit is), so that it can be used as a private key, or part of a private key, for the sake of encryption.\n"
"But in this lesson the focus is on entanglement and a few things we can do with it.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa puntong ito, maaari rin nating tingnan ang probabilistic state $(2)$ bilang isang resource, na kumakatawan sa isang bit ng *shared randomness*.\n"
"Maaari itong maging lubhang kapaki-pakinabang sa cryptography, halimbawa, na magbahagi ng isang random na bit sa isang tao (sa pag-aakala na walang iba ang nakakaalam kung ano ang bit), upang magamit ito bilang isang private key, o bahagi ng isang private key, para sa layunin ng encryption.\n"
"Ngunit sa araling ito ang pokus ay sa entanglement at ilang bagay na maaari nating gawin dito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As a point of clarification regarding terminology, when we say that Alice and Bob *share an e-bit*, what we mean is that Alice has a qubit named $\\mathsf{A},$ Bob has a qubit named $\\mathsf{B},$ and together the pair $(\\mathsf{A},\\mathsf{B})$ is in the quantum state $(1).$\n"
"Different names could, of course, be chosen for the qubits, but throughout this lesson we will stick with these names in the interest of clarity.\n"
msgstr ""
"Bilang paglilinaw tungkol sa terminolohiya, kapag sinabi nating *nagbabahagi si Alice at Bob ng isang e-bit*, ang ibig sabihin nito ay mayroon si Alice ng isang qubit na pinangalanang $\\mathsf{A},$ si Bob ay mayroon ng isang qubit na pinangalanang $\\mathsf{B},$ at magkasama ang pares $(\\mathsf{A},\\mathsf{B})$ ay nasa quantum state $(1).$\n"
"Maaaring pumili ng iba pang mga pangalan para sa mga qubit, ngunit sa buong araling ito mananatili tayo sa mga pangalang ito para sa kalinawan.\n"
